// This is deliberately a single query with no cross-project fallback. The
// same GitHub upstream can have more than one live placement sharing a
// repo_group (e.g. the same owner mirrored into two different clusters,
// each its own project) — only the placement under the group's own
// owning_project_id resolves here; a sibling placement in another project
// simply isn't considered that project's repo anymore. The physical
// placement keeps working for git operations regardless (clone/push/serving
// key off cluster_slug/jurisdiction, untouched by this); it just won't
// surface when someone looks up or lists repos scoped to that other
// project, until each GitHub upstream is consolidated onto a single
// project for real.
// Queries DISTINCT repo_group_id, not a single row: multi-cluster mirroring
// (COR-1265 item 2/7) means the SAME logical repo can hold more than one
// repo_registry placement sharing this project and name — that must resolve,
// not ambiguity-error. Genuine ambiguity is two or more DIFFERENT repo_groups
// matching (project, lower(name)): dropping the placement-level
// (owning_project_id, lower(name)) unique indexes (migrations 038/046,
// 012/025) to permit that multi-cluster case also, as a side effect, permits
